{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794994446,"nonce":"fxQYM93JDnE/mfEaLiRarJlTRI4=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDORPa0Bs8AAAABgUrcaQ","github-url":"https://github.com/FhG-IISB/jNO/issues/203","origin":"github"},"title":"Tutorial inverse_wave_speed_2d generates its data with private functions","message":"`docs/tutorial_examples/08_fem_and_varpinns/inverse_wave_speed_2d.py` generates its observed data, and the start and recovered trajectories, with two private functions:\n\n```python\nfrom jno.utils.solver.backend_blocks import _block_time_grid, _default_transient_integrate\n\nblk = fem.operator\nts = np.asarray(_block_time_grid(blk))\nu_obs = np.asarray(_default_transient_integrate(blk, {\"c2\": c2_true}, ts))\n```\n\nPresent on release 0.4.2 and on `main` (b3dcdd22).\n\nThe public interface already gives the same trajectory. With the tutorial's term list:\n\n```python\nu_obs = jnp.asarray(fem.solve(c2=jnp.array([c2_true])))\n```\n\nChecked on 0.4.2: with the data generated this way, the recovery of the wave speed through `jno.core([(fem.solve() - u_obs).mse])` converges as in the tutorial. On four meshes (sizes 0.14 to 0.05, 60 to 180 time levels) the relative error of `c2` after 220 Adam iterations was between 4.2e-6 and 5.4e-7.\n\n## Request\n\nReplace the private calls in the tutorial by `fem.solve(c2=...)`, so that a reader who copies it uses only public names.","files":null}]}